## Source and method

Hayman–Lingham printed65, Problem3.16 and its update were visually inspected. The exact closed-ball Newtonian capacity integral and n>=3 are retained. Hedberg–Wolff's Borel-set proof of Theorem2, pp173–174, was read for its capacitary-measure and small-integral contradiction strategy. That strategy is explicitly credited. The 2018 editors' 'no progress reported' sentence is not misrepresented as an exhaustive literature conclusion.

## Analytical audit

Every section of TURN_1 was checked. Compact finite-energy minimizers exist by weak compactness and lower semicontinuity; restrictions have finite cross energy and justify the first variation. Variation implies U>=J almost everywhere, and its energy integral gives equality. Lower semicontinuity has the correct direction: approximating support points by a dense equality set gives U<=J on support. The nearest-support distance inequality yields the global 2^s J bound without importing an equilibrium quasi-everywhere theorem. Restricted probability normalization is m², giving precisely mu(A)<=2^s J cap(A).

The closed-ball compact intersections have upper-semicontinuous capacities under center/radius convergence: containment in slightly enlarged limiting balls and continuity on decreasing compacts prove this. Parameter integration is Borel. The finiteness set is therefore Borel, enabling ordinary Choquet capacitability. A finite-energy measure, measurable small-tail exhaustion, countable small-cube cover and inner regularity give an actual compact positive-capacity subset with the tail bound at every point. No unjustified uniform convergence on all thin points is assumed.

Tonelli gives the layer-cake identity even on the infinite diagonal. Applying restriction domination below delta and total mass above it yields U<=J/4+delta^(-s). The diameter lower bound on J and diam(K)<=delta/4 give the second term<=J/4, because s=n-2>=1. This contradicts the measure-almost-everywhere equality U=J>0. All quantifiers and scales match the original integral.

Borel capacitability and capacity-zero/polar equivalence remain explicit standard foundations. They are not the fine-topological Choquet property, Kellogg theorem or Wiener boundary regularity criterion. Their use is noncircular relative to the requested direct argument. This review does not claim to re-prove foundational potential theory.

## Sharpness

Weakening the denominator exponent by any positive amount makes the integral finite at every point by the ball-capacity upper bound; a ball has positive capacity by finite volume-measure energy. The dyadic line-segment argument is correct, including endpoints handled by a disjoint interval partition: same-bin pairs have distance at most the bin diameter and squared masses sum to at least the reciprocal number of bins. Its infinite energy makes the uncountable segment polar. These establish critical power and uncountable exceptional-set sharpness, not an unformulated universal statement for every nonintegrable gauge.

